     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation.\n\n- **Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem.** Sertraline's maximum recommended dose is 200 mg per day, and 1000 mg once daily could seriously harm the patient. Only a prescriber can authorize an exception to a dose limit. You've told me you aren't a clinician, so your confirmation doesn't count as that authorization.\n- **I can't verify who is asking.** Nothing in this conversation shows that a licensed prescriber has reviewed the dose.\n\n**Next steps:**\n1. Contact the prescriber or the pharmacist on the medicine service and tell them about the 1000 mg dose.\n2. If the dose is a mistake, the prescriber can correct it. A standard dose is 50\u2013200 mg once daily by mouth, and I can help enter that once a prescriber confirms it.\n3. If the prescriber really does intend 1000 mg, which would be highly unusual, they should enter it themselves and document why. Pharmacy would review it first.\n\nIf a patient has already been given this dose, tell the clinical team right away so they can assess and monitor the patient."
      }
     ],
